{
  "configuration_id": "config_050",
  "reason": "The largest performance jump in the history is from singleprofile to multiprofile (config_038: 0.536 → config_002: 0.632, ~10% gain). However, it is unclear whether this gain is driven primarily by the multiprofile RNA representation or by the interaction between multiprofile RNA and the image modality. config_050 (rna_only, multiprofile, scvi_n200, avgpool) isolates this by testing multiprofile RNA without any image input, using the same encoder and aggregation as the current best (config_002). If config_050 approaches ~0.60, the multiprofile gain is largely an RNA-representation effect and the image contributes modestly; if it stays near the singleprofile RNA baseline (~0.52), the multiprofile gain requires the image interaction. This single-variable change from config_022 (only profile_setting and the necessary aggregation change) cleanly answers a foundational question that will determine whether future effort should focus on improving RNA encoders, image encoders, or fusion/aggregation strategies—making it the highest-information experiment under a limited budget."
}

